{"text": "Miami Herald\n\nThe Miami Herald (Miami, Florida) - 19 Nov 1989, Sun: Page 216\n\nPrinted on Jun 29, 2021\n\nwens eee eer mene\n\nBy ANN SIMMONS\nHerald Staff Writer\nEight oo mee construction\nstopped in the initial stages on three\nigh-rise condominiums in Surfside,\none of the developers says he is\n\nready to proceed with his 12-story\n\nProchgh some Surfside residents\n\nangrily denounced the plans to res-\nurrect the Centennial Towers pro-\nject at 8855 Collins Ave., commis-\nsioners voted 3-2 to give \u2018developer\nNathan Reiber the go-ahead. In fa-\nvor were Commissioners Ben Le-\nvine, Eli Lurie and Mickey Novack;\ndissenting were Mayor Mitchell\npss and Vice ayer Eli Tourge-\n\net ee ee ee eee eee ere\n\nSurfside lets condo |\n\n\u201cHow better to fill up a dirty and\nugly hole than with a beautiful build-\ning in $100,000 [in proper-\nty taxes] and more to the town of\nSurfside \u2014 and bringing in 100 new\nfamilies to shop in our business dis-\ntrict, which sorely needs a shot in\nthe arm,\u201d Levine said Wednesday.\nResidents were unconvinced.\nSome shouted down Reiber and im-\nlored commissioners to require\nReiber to put up an unconditional\nbond of $500,000 to ensure the\ntown would have money to fill in the\nhole if the property is abandoned.\n\u201cThis has been going on and on\nand I think it\u2019s time you commis-\nsioners did something aboit it,\u201d said\nSurfside resident Saul Gromet.\n\nAT 5:30 P.M., THE TIES COME\n\nTS GO ON.\n\nWhen it's time to start a healthy new\n\nflifestyle. do it right. At Pritikin. Get back in shape.\n\nClipped By:\n\npbeall1\nTue, Jun 29, 2021\n\n&\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2021 Newspapers.com. All Rights Reserved.\n\n\u201cThe problem seems to become a\nmore irksome and troublesome one\nas time goes by. There\u2019s no reason\nwhy we who live in Surfside should\nput sy ca with this,\u201d said civic activist\n\n\u201cTt\u2019s disgusting,\u201d said Doug Hal-\nstead, manager of Champlain Tow-\ners North, 8877 Collins Ave., next\ndoor to the Centennial Towers site.\nBh definitely nothing that\u2019s com-\nlementary to the town of Surfside.\nfe don\u2019t feel [Reiber] is a very co-\noperative neighbor.\u201d\nvite us seul Florida condo\nmarket took a dive eight years\n~ Reiber and the developers of of the\nsey Bal Colony and Richelieu\n\u2018owers bailed out, leaving behind\nconcrete-lined holes where the\nee. foundations had been\n\n7 city ordered the Centennial\nto chop down overgrown\npines at the site. But left behind\nwere old oil drums, pieces of lumber\n\nproject resume\n\nand construction debris, and pools of\nmosquito-attracting water in the el-\nevator shafts and pool excavation\nsites.\nOn Tuesday, commissioners gave\nReiber permission to build closer to\nthe property line than town laws\nnormally allow.\n\nReiber, who has started to clean\nup the site, told commissioners and\nthe angry residents that he would\ncontinue with construction as soon\nas he gets the required coastal con-\nstruction permit from the state De-\npartment of Natural Resources.\n\nTown Manager Hal Cohen ac-\nknowledged that residents are fed\nup with the \u201c\u2018eyesores,\u201d He said de- .\nvelopers of the Bal Colony, 9455\nCollins Ave., also are waiting for a\nstate permit to resume construc-\ntion. But continued work on Riche-\nlieu Towers, 8925 Collins Ave.,\nlooks doubtful, Cohen said, so the\nsite \u201cneeds to be closed up.\u201d\n\nNewspapers",
